9689	9865	9663	9663	herself	110	" My dear Love,"--she wrote,--"I can not remain here to see you after the discovery which has been made to - day . I am a miserable coward ; and I can not meet your altered looks , I can not hear your altered voice . I have no hope that you can have any other feeling for me than contempt and loathing . But on some future day , when I am far away from you , and the bewilderment of my present misery has grown less , I will write and explain everything . Think of me mercifully , if you can ; and if you can believe that , in the wicked concealments of the last few weeks , the mainspring of my conduct has been my love for you , you will only believe the truth . God bless you , my best and truest . The pain of leaving you for ever is less than the pain of knowing that you had ceased to love me . Good - bye . "
